WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:11.950
Four years ago, I survived one of the
worst natural disasters on record.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.675
It was January 12th, 2010 and I was in, in
a meeting with my colleagues in

00:00:17.675 --> 00:00:22.420
Port-au-Prince, Haiti, when the walls of
our building started to shake.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.150
Scared and

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:28.120
confused, we raced to the door, only to
realize that the door was jammed shut.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:29.970
We were trapped.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.330
When the shaking stopped, we unjammed the
door, and we raced down the stairs.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.370
And as I stepped over the threshold,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.570
I was welcomed by the outstretched arm of
my colleague.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:48.800
And then I looked up, and he was covered
in dust and white powder.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.010
And I started to look around.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.350
And to my left, the cultural center,
vaporized.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.190
The primary school in the far corner,
collapsed.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:10.210
The building I had just come out of was
hanging by a thread.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.380
And I grasped the hand of my colleague and

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.970
we looked out onto the city that we had
called home.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.240
And it was, barely recognizable.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:29.030
The next day, I was completely overwhelmed
by what I saw.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:37.180
Collapsed homes and hospitals, families
separated, a country rocked to its core.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.630
And I had this feeling of knots in my
stomach.

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:47.380
I wanted to do something, but the problem
seemed so

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:51.170
much bigger than me, and I didn't know
where to start.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:56.100
I felt, helpless.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:04.470
In that same moment on the other side of
town, my colleague

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:09.550
Paul was emerging from the rubble of his
collapsed four story building.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.950
And instead of succumbing to the feeling
of helplessness that I felt,

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.330
he was compelled to take action.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:22.820
And that's what I want to talk to you
about today.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.850
I learned a profound lesson from Paul that
day.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:35.100
It's about what I can do, and what you can
do in the face of overwhelming challenges.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:41.040
Paul was a 24 year old who worked with me
at

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.320
the Microfinance Bank I worked at in
Haiti.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:48.200
He joined our organization because he was
really passionate about our

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.570
mission to deliver financial and

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:54.180
educational resources to some of the most
vulnerable communities in Haiti.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:57.720
He had been quite successful, and had been
known for

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.660
developing a really strong relationship
with his clients.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:08.805
Paul was in his apartment building when
the earthquake hit that day and

00:03:08.805 --> 00:03:11.261
collapsed with him inside.

00:03:11.261 --> 00:03:17.965
For 24 hours, he was trapped beneath the
rubble of his home,

00:03:17.965 --> 00:03:22.280
until his neighbors came to dig him out.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.230
In that moment, Paul had a choice.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.400
He could have let the situation overwhelm
and paralyze him.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:42.616
But instead, injured and dehydrated, he
took a stick and he started walking.

00:03:42.616 --> 00:03:52.230
And step by step, he walked to the home of
his first client to see if they were okay.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:57.440
And then, he walked to the home of his
second client, and

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.570
then the third, and then the fourth.

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:06.329
And over and over, step by step, door by
door, house by house,

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:13.277
Paul went to check on each and every one
of his clients to see if they were okay.

00:04:13.277 --> 00:04:16.597
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:04:16.597 --> 00:04:21.650
The steps Paul took that day had a huge
impact.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:25.920
For months, stories rolled in from his
clients with thank yous saying how

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.000
Paul's visit that day brought them the
hope they needed in

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.520
a moment of uncertainty and despair.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.230
Because of Paul, they knew they were not
alone and forgotten.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:42.880
And that hope led them to take their first
steps.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:45.400
From finding a tarp for

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:50.150
their roof, to picking fruit from a nearby
tree to pool with their neighbors.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:54.580
Or finally just having the confidence to
tell their children that everything was

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:55.380
going to be okay.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:02.340
Paul had a ripple effect, and when the
stories of him and others like him who

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:06.870
took small steps that day started to roll
in, things started to move.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:12.310
At my organization, one of our senior
executives started emailing and

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.350
calling all of our 900 plus employees.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:19.410
A small group of colleagues showed up to
our central office building the next day

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:22.700
and started salvaging what they could.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:27.600
And I finally showered and left my house
for the first time in three days.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:34.070
We went on to accomplish more than I ever
could have imagined in the weeks and

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:35.580
months following the earthquake.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:40.880
I have thought a lot about what Paul did
that day.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.130
I think about the fear and paralysis that
I felt, and I think about the strength and

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:46.360
courage that he demonstrated.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:50.290
And I wonder, what did he know that I
didn't?

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:55.760
How did he find the strength and courage
he needed to take action in that moment?

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:03.440
So one day I asked him, and he responded,
tipa tipa, madame.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.120
Step by step, madame.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.430
He knew he needed to do something.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:15.790
He decided on a path, and then he took it
step by step.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.440
And one door led to two, which led to
three, and things started to spread.

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:27.480
Now this expression, tipa tipa, it,

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.080
hearing it from Paul was not the first
time I heard it, actually.

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:35.930
Growing up with a Haitian father, I
remember numerous times when I was facing

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:42.865
an obstacle or struggle, he would tell me,
tipa tipa, Natalie, step by step.

00:06:42.865 --> 00:06:47.190
And then, I think about my mother, who's
French Canadian.

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:49.870
And when she was struggling with learning
English.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:55.810
And her mother would tell her, [FOREIGN],
step by step.

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:57.773
And then my husband who's from Senegal,

00:06:57.773 --> 00:07:01.147
when he was coaching his friend who was
going through a difficult time.

00:07:01.147 --> 00:07:07.400
He used an expression in his native
language, [FOREIGN], or step by step.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:14.150
Across continents and cultures, everywhere
I've looked, I found this unifying theme

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:18.170
of using the concept of step by step to
get through a difficult time.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.980
So what did Paul do?

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:26.450
Paul took an age old notion, a universal
piece of wisdom,

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:30.470
and he put it into practice in a powerful
way.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:40.150
Because Paul knew, taking things step by
step is the only way to get things done.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:44.370
So why is this important to us here today?

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:52.620
I've had conversations with many of you in
this room, who want to solve problems or

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:56.990
change a situation, and sometimes we feel
overwhelmed.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:00.160
We're not sure where to start or which
path to take.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.290
We worry about the impact we'll have, and
we get stuck.

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:10.060
So I ask you, when's the last time you
felt overwhelmed by a situation?

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:16.420
For me, it was this past January 12th on
the four year anniversary of

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:17.010
the earthquake.

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:21.880
Well I was proud of the things we
accomplished through news reports and

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:23.570
conversations with friends,

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:25.850
I was made aware that there's still a lot
of work left to do.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:29.050
And I was wondering, what can I do from
here?

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:34.720
And those knots in my stomach started to
come back.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.230
And then, I remembered Paul and I took my
first step.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:40.170
I sent an email.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:47.040
And that email turned into one, then
three, then five and

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:52.120
more of our classmates, signing up to join
me on a trip to Haiti this spring break.

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:56.670
And this trip will be their first steps in
understanding and

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:01.560
learning about the opportunities and
obstacles that Haiti faces.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:05.470
So for me, the first steps was an e-mail.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:10.690
For the aspiring entrepreneur who's
sitting on an idea, take your first steps.

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:11.820
Make that phone call.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:12.540
Share your idea.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:18.940
For the aspiring runner who's wanting to
get ready for the next big race.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:21.450
Take your first step and put on your
running shoes.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:26.730
For the new parents who are worried if
they're gonna do a good job,

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:33.020
take the first step and believe that you
will.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:39.183
So, I ask of you, the next time you're
feeling overwhelmed,

00:09:39.183 --> 00:09:46.411
remember tipa tipa and let this wisdom of
step by step take you through it.

00:09:46.411 --> 00:09:52.281
Because, overcoming life's challenges can

00:09:52.281 --> 00:09:56.651
be as easy as taking a single step.

00:09:56.651 --> 00:10:02.229
[MUSIC]

